<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="beb80851">&lt;p&gt;
    Identifying stakeholders along with their anticipated interests, priorities, and level of influence is an important
    aspect of planning a project. Managing stakeholders&amp;nbsp;is also&amp;nbsp;an important aspect of project delivery, as it is
    fundamental to the three elements of Service Quality: Meeting the requirements of all stakeholders, in accordance with
    professional and EY standards; Delivering superior service to clients; and Delivering services consistently across
    time, geographies and service lines. Stakeholder management helps us to understand the client’s needs and expectations,
    and provides an opportunity for us to develop our relationship and deliver on Service Quality. It also helps our client
    sponsor understand the level of internal commitment required to make our collective efforts a success.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Stakeholders are individuals or groups who have an interest in the outcome of the project. Stakeholders may participate
    directly in the project, or they may be affected by the result of the project (e.g., an end user who needs to learn a
    new software application). Stakeholders often have important influence. The influence, based on a combination of power
    and interest, can be positive, neutral, or negative. Identifying stakeholders along with their anticipated interests,
    priorities, and level of influence is an important aspect of planning a project. Addressing stakeholder requirements
    often directly influences the project approach, communications, risk management, and benefits components of the project
    plan.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Stakeholder Identification&lt;br /&gt;
    The first step is working with the client sponsor to identify and list the stakeholders who are directly or indirectly
    impacted by the project. This requires considering how the objectives and scope of the project relate to stakeholders.
    Typical stakeholders include senior executives, line managers, staff, customers, suppliers, shareholders or other
    interest groups. When identifying key stakeholders, consider the following:&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    People whose behavior needs to change as a result of the project&lt;br /&gt;
    People who control resources / commodities needed to carry out or change the project&lt;br /&gt;
    People with power / influence that may be redistributed&lt;br /&gt;
    People who may benefit or lose directly or indirectly as a result of a change, or outcome of the project&lt;br /&gt;
    Organization or agencies who influence the direct or indirect stakeholders&lt;br /&gt;
    Stakeholders may display different characteristics depending on the nature of the project, for example, in the case of
    a change program, the extent of change and associated impact may be greater than in other projects, which could
    increase stakeholder resistance. It is important to be aware of the characteristics below, which may also help identify
    key stakeholders. These considerations need framing according to the nature of the project (e.g., for “Power” we would
    consider who has the power to legitimize or influence change or the success of the project):&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Power – The individual or organizational power to legitimize the change within the organization&lt;br /&gt;
    Pain – A personal stake in making the change succeed, to the extent that the current situation is more painful/costly
    than the change&lt;br /&gt;
    Vision – A total, in-depth understanding of what change must occur and the effect the change will have on the
    organization&lt;br /&gt;
    Public Role – The willingness and ability to demonstrate commitment to and active public support for the
    change/project.&lt;br /&gt;
    Private Role – The willingness and ability to meet privately with agents to discuss progress, problems and concerns
    with those impacted by the change/project.&lt;br /&gt;
    Performance Management – The willingness and ability to reward desired behaviors and confront undesirable behaviors
    regarding the change.&lt;br /&gt;
    Sacrifice – The commitment to pursue the change despite the personal or political consequences.&lt;br /&gt;
    Managing Stakeholder Expectation&lt;br /&gt;
    A project’s stakeholder management strategy helps provide a focus for gathering and understanding stakeholder
    expectations and needs. This is essential as stakeholders: \&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Influence the execution and outcome of projects&lt;br /&gt;
    Are integral to effective planning and project delivery&lt;br /&gt;
    Measure our performance against the achievement of their expectations&lt;br /&gt;
    The project team interviews key stakeholders to determine their needs; however this may not always be practical or
    possible in the case of groups or external stakeholders. In this scenario, we consider the need for desk-based research
    that anticipates their needs. When interviewing stakeholders consider the following factors as an example of topics to
    cover:&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Expected outcome from the project&lt;br /&gt;
    Specific needs or focus areas&lt;br /&gt;
    Known issues or problems associated with areas in the scope of work&lt;br /&gt;
    The stakeholders measure of success&lt;br /&gt;
    Preferred communication channel and approach&lt;br /&gt;
    If the stakeholder interview reveals a variance between what the stakeholder expects and what the project can deliver,
    we work with the client sponsor to take appropriate action. This may result in a need to issue an addendum to the
    engagement letter in order to align our scope of work more closely with stakeholder expectations. It may also result in
    the need for the client sponsor to take direct action.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
